# The ledger of `property`: what property-based testing lets a test do, one
# block per capability, and what Rebel answers. The lists are this machine's,
# 2026-11-19, read and never their source: Go's `testing/quick` (`go doc
# -all`, Go 1.27.3); Rust's `proptest` 1.12.0 and `quickcheck` 1.1.0, fetched
# into the scratch folder and read from `cargo doc` (their items and every
# method of `Strategy`, `TestRunner`, `Config`, `QuickCheck`, `Gen` and
# `TestResult`), Rust's standard library holding none; Python's `hypothesis`
# 6.170.15, installed into the scratch folder and read by `dir()` of
# `hypothesis`, `hypothesis.strategies`, `hypothesis.settings` and
# `hypothesis.stateful`. Haskell's QuickCheck is the reference the task names
# and no Haskell is on this machine, so its line is the export list of
# `Test.QuickCheck` 2.19.0.0 as Hackage's documentation gives it, read
# 2026-11-19: a list read, never run.
#
# The shape every block rests on: Heroes has no closure, and a function value
# takes no `@` parameter, so a generator is a top-level function the program
# writes, `(function(Source) -> T?)`, drawing every part of its value from one
# `Source` through the draws below. Its value is a function of one sequence of
# choices, which the package keeps, replays and shrinks: Hypothesis's
# integrated shrinking (its "internal reduction"), never a shrinker per type.

capability property/check  a property checked over many generated cases, answering `bool`
  heroes  property.check
  go      quick.Check, quick.Config.Values
  rust    proptest::proptest!, proptest::test_runner::TestRunner, TestRunner::run, TestRunner::run_one, TestRunner::new, TestRunner::default, quickcheck::quickcheck, quickcheck::quickcheck!, quickcheck::QuickCheck, QuickCheck::new, QuickCheck::quickcheck, QuickCheck::quicktest, quickcheck::Testable, Testable::result
  python  hypothesis.given
  haskell quickCheck, quickCheckWith, quickCheckResult, quickCheckWithResult, property, Property, Testable, forAll, forAllShow, forAllBlind, propertyForAllShrinkShow
  note    Go's `quick.Check` and QuickCheck take a function of typed arguments and find generators by reflection or a type class; here the generator is named beside the property, `(function(Source) -> T?)`, and the report needs `show` to write the case, the language having no rendering of a record

capability property/check-result  a property that answers its own failure in words
  heroes  property.check_result
  go      -
  rust    proptest::prop_assert!, proptest::prop_assert_eq!, proptest::prop_assert_ne!, TestCaseError::Fail, TestCaseError::fail, TestCaseResult, ProptestResultExt, Reason, quickcheck::TestResult, TestResult::passed, TestResult::failed, TestResult::error, TestResult::from_bool, TestResult::is_failure, TestResult::is_error
  python  hypothesis.note
  haskell counterexample, printTestCase, whenFail, whenFail', (===), (=/=), total, ioProperty, idempotentIOProperty
  note    a failure's code and message are the property's words in the report, as `prop_assert!` and `counterexample` let a property say why; shrinking keeps only candidates failing with the first failure's code, as Hypothesis shrinks each distinct failure apart

capability property/check-equal  two functions answering alike on every generated case
  heroes  property.check_equal
  go      quick.CheckEqual, quick.CheckEqualError
  rust    -
  python  -
  haskell (===)
  note    the answers are compared by the program's `same`, since a type parameter's `==` is refused by the compiler (a `partial` record may arrive), and the report shows the case, not the two answers

capability property/outcome  what a run found, told as values and as a paragraph
  heroes  property.Outcome, property.Verdict, property.Failure
  go      quick.CheckError, quick.SetupError
  rust    proptest::test_runner::TestError, TestError::Fail, TestError::Abort
  python  -
  haskell Result, Success, GaveUp, Failure, NoExpectedFailure, isSuccess
  note    QuickCheck's `NoExpectedFailure` is a test asserting `.failed`; `report` is `HELD` alone where the property held, so `assert outcome.report == property.HELD` prints the whole report when it did not

capability property/expect-failure  a property a test expects to fail
  heroes  property.Verdict
  go      -
  rust    quickcheck::TestResult::must_fail
  python  -
  haskell expectFailure
  note    a test asserts the verdict `.failed`, its smallest case read from the `Failure`

capability property/seed  a run reproduced from the seed its failure prints
  heroes  property.seeded
  go      quick.Config.Rand
  rust    proptest::test_runner::Config::rng_seed, RngSeed, RngSeed::Fixed, TestRunner::deterministic, TestRunner::new_with_rng, TestRunner::new_rng, TestRunner::rng, TestRng, quickcheck::Gen::from_size_and_seed, QuickCheck::rng, QuickCheck::gen
  python  hypothesis.seed, hypothesis.settings.derandomize
  haskell replay, recheck
  note    every case is drawn from PCG (`random.Generator`) seeded by the run's seed and the case's number, so a seed and a generator make the same run on every machine; the report names the seed

capability property/fresh-seed  a run seeded from the system's generator
  heroes  property.seeded_by_system
  go      -
  rust    RngSeed::Random
  python  -
  haskell -

capability property/cases  how many cases must hold
  heroes  property.with_cases
  go      quick.Config.MaxCount, quick.Config.MaxCountScale
  rust    Config::cases, Config::with_cases, QuickCheck::tests, QuickCheck::max_tests, QuickCheck::min_tests_passed
  python  hypothesis.settings.max_examples
  haskell maxSuccess, withMaxSuccess, withNumTests, once, again
  note    100 by default, as Go's `quick`, Hypothesis (`max_examples`, read 2026-11-19) and QuickCheck default; proptest runs 256 (its `Config` documentation)

capability property/rejects  how many cases may be rejected before the run gives up
  heroes  property.with_rejects
  go      -
  rust    Config::max_local_rejects, Config::max_global_rejects, TestRunner::reject_local
  python  hypothesis.HealthCheck.filter_too_much
  haskell maxDiscardRatio, withDiscardRatio
  note    1,000 by default, ten for each case asked, QuickCheck's `maxDiscardRatio` as its documentation gives it; past it the verdict is `gave_up` with the last rejection's words

capability property/shrink-bound  how much work shrinking may do, none included
  heroes  property.with_shrinks
  go      -
  rust    Config::max_shrink_iters, Strategy::no_shrink, proptest::strategy::NoShrink, quickcheck::NoShrink
  python  hypothesis.settings.phases, hypothesis.Phase.shrink
  haskell maxShrinks, withMaxShrinks, noShrinking
  note    a bound of candidates tried, 2,000 by default, 0 keeping the case as drawn; proptest bounds the same count (`max_shrink_iters`), QuickCheck the steps kept

capability property/size-bound  how large one case may grow
  heroes  property.with_choices
  go      -
  rust    Config::max_default_size_range, proptest::collection::size_range, SizeRange
  python  hypothesis.HealthCheck.data_too_large
  haskell maxSize, withMaxSize, sized, getSize, resize, scale, mapSize
  note    a bound of the choices one case draws, 8,192 by default (Hypothesis's `BUFFER_SIZE` reads 8192, read 2026-11-19), past which a draw fails `limit`; QuickCheck's size that grows with the case number is not kept, a list's length being set by `many` and the first cases being the simplest and the edges

capability property/shrinking  the failing case reduced to a smallest one
  heroes  property.check, property.check_result, property.check_equal, property.smallest
  go      -
  rust    proptest::strategy::ValueTree, quickcheck::Arbitrary::shrink, quickcheck::empty_shrinker, quickcheck::single_shrinker
  python  hypothesis.Phase.shrink, hypothesis.Phase.explain
  haskell shrink, shrinkList, shrinkMap, shrinkMapBy, shrinkIntegral, shrinkRealFrac, shrinkBoundedEnum, shrinkDecimal, shrinkNothing, genericShrink, subterms, recursivelyShrink, forAllShrink, forAllShrinkShow, forAllShrinkBlind, shrinking, Shrinking, ShrinkState, shrinkInit, shrinkState, Shrink2, getShrink2
  note    integrated shrinking as Hypothesis does it, on the sequence of choices the generator drew: the choices are cut to the fewest that fail, blocks deleted and zeroed, a choice and the next made 0 and the largest, equal choices lowered together, each choice lowered by halving, neighbours swapped, a choice moved onto the next; a candidate is kept where it is smaller (fewer choices, then lower) and fails alike. No shrinker is written per type, so the program's records shrink with no code of its own

capability property/assume  a case rejected, by its generator or by the property
  heroes  property.assume
  go      -
  rust    proptest::prop_assume!, TestCaseError::Reject, TestCaseError::reject, quickcheck::TestResult::discard
  python  hypothesis.assume, hypothesis.reject
  haskell (==>), discard, Discard
  note    a rejection is a failure `invalid` from the generator or a `()?` property, counted against `rejects` and never as a failure

