capability property/integers  whole numbers in a range, its edges first
  heroes  property.int_between, property.u64_between, property.ints
  go      -
  rust    proptest::num::i8::ANY, i16::ANY, i32::ANY, i64::ANY, i128::ANY, u8::ANY, u16::ANY, u32::ANY, u64::ANY, u128::ANY, isize::ANY, usize::ANY, proptest::num::sample_uniform_incl, proptest::bits::i64::between, proptest::bits::u64::between
  python  hypothesis.strategies.integers
  haskell choose, chooseInt, chooseInteger, chooseWord64, chooseInt64, chooseUpTo, chooseBoundedIntegral, chooseAny, arbitrarySizedIntegral, arbitrarySizedNatural, arbitrarySizedBoundedIntegral, arbitraryBoundedIntegral, arbitraryBoundedRandom, Positive, Negative, NonZero, NonNegative, NonPositive, Large, Small
  note    the edges first: the first case draws every number at its target (0, or the end nearer 0), the next tenth of the cases draw edges only (the target, 1, -1, the ends and their neighbours), the rest an edge one draw in eight, as Hypothesis mixes its boundary values in; both ends are included, as Hypothesis's `integers` and QuickCheck's `choose`, where `random.int_in` leaves its end out. The side above the target is the simpler: where both sides fail the smallest case is the least failing number above it, 256 for a byte's range where Hypothesis's `find` answers -1 (run 2026-11-19), so that halving a magnitude ends on a threshold on either side; and a choice past the end reads 0, so of two numbers whose sum must reach 1000 the smallest case is (1000, 0), where Hypothesis answers (0, 1000). For a threshold, a duplicate in a list, a character in a text and a number past 1000 the two answer alike: 1000, [0, 0], `é` and 1001

capability property/floats  floats in a range, and any float with the special values
  heroes  property.float, property.float_between, property.floats
  go      -
  rust    proptest::num::f64::ANY, f64::POSITIVE, f64::NEGATIVE, f64::NORMAL, f64::SUBNORMAL, f64::ZERO, f64::INFINITE, f64::QUIET_NAN, f64::SIGNALING_NAN, the same of f32
  python  hypothesis.strategies.floats
  haskell arbitrarySizedFractional
  note    `float` draws every special value among its edges, nan, both infinities and both zeros included, as Hypothesis's `floats()`; `float_between` draws finite floats only, its ends included

